These five headers are Amazon's, copied verbatim and not edited.

  Source:  FreeBSD src, sys/contrib/ena-com/ena_defs/
  Fetched: 2026-09-14 from raw.githubusercontent.com/freebsd/freebsd-src/main
  Licence: BSD-3-Clause, Copyright (c) 2015-2023 Amazon.com, Inc.

The same files, byte for byte, are in DPDK's drivers/net/ena/base/ena_defs/
and in amzn-drivers' kernel/fbsd/ena/ena-com/, all BSD-3-Clause. They are
machine-generated from Amazon's internal ABI description, which is exactly
why they are used as they are rather than transcribed into constants of our
own: every offset, every bit position and every enumerator in the device
interface lives here, and each one retyped by hand is a chance to be wrong
in a way that produces no error.

That is not a hypothetical. The gVNIC model in this tree was written from
hand-copied offsets and had four of them wrong -- a field read four bytes
early that found a plausible zero, a receive descriptor's length and
sequence written two bytes early so that the driver read its sequence
number out of bytes nobody had written and concluded, silently and forever,
that no packet had ever arrived. ENA has a worse trap of the same kind
waiting: a transmit descriptor's req_id is split across two fields, six bits
in one and ten in another.

To update, refetch the whole directory from one of the three sources above
and change nothing. Anything this model needs that the headers do not
provide belongs in ena.c.

BSD-3-Clause sources are compatible with QEMU's GPL-2.0-or-later; the
combined work is GPL, and these files keep their own notices.
